  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1204 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nUnseasonably mild conditions will continue Tuesday into \nWednesday, as we see weak ridging and warm advection ahead of an\napproaching wave. Daytime temperatures in the low 60s can be \nexpected for most on Tuesday, with some passing clouds early and\nlight winds. Going into Wednesday, the aforementioned wave \npasses overhead, bringing increasing cloud cover and \nprecipitation chances. Confidence on rainfall is low-medium, \nowing to uncertainty on moisture availability. Although, a look \nat model soundings would indicate that we might saturate enough \nfor light showers, but it might take some time through the day \nfor it. Thus, current PoPs might be a little ambitious on the \nrainfall chances earlier in the day. Compared to the last \nforecast package, we have seen an increase in PoPs with the NBM,\nwith best chances now generally along/north of Interstate 80. \nWhile the chances may have increased (30-50%), we are expecting \nlight rainfall with low accumulation. WPC continues to favor \n<0.10\" for those seeing light rain. Otherwise, a mostly cloud \nday can be expected with temperatures in the mid-upper 50s. \n\nThis system will be quick to move out, with notable cold advection \nkicking in on the back end. Not only will dry air work back into the \narea, but we will also see much cooler air work in. Granted, we\nwill remain near seasonal norms in the low-mid 40s, but these \nare cooler daytime temps than we have seen over the last few \nweeks. Breezy gradient winds will also kick in on the back-end\nof the system Thursday, resulting in a chilly breeze. High \npressure will then settle into the region late this week and \ninto the weekend, bringing a period of cooler and quiet weather.",
